In C++, you can achieve this by utilizing lambda functions or function objects if you want to encapsulate the same logic into a reusable function. Here's a concise way to do it:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <functional>

// Example function to demonstrate the concept
void processVariable(int& var) {
    std::cout << "Processing variable: " << var << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    int a = 5, b = 10, c = 15;

    // Define a lambda function that wraps the processVariable function
    auto process = [](int& var) { processVariable(var); };

    // Apply the lambda function to each variable
    process(a); // Processing variable: 5
    process(b); // Processing variable: 10
    process(c); // Processing variable: 15

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `process` lambda function is defined to encapsulate the logic of the `processVariable` function. This allows you to run the same code on multiple named variables by simply calling the lambda function with each variable.

